{
  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 0 (R2A): all 60 trials across oval, s-bend and slalom ended off_track, with progress of only 0.04 to 0.12, one line_loss_event per run, and max_error about 0.061 m. That is just past the 0.06 m off-track limit. steering_oscillation (0.06 to 0.07) and rms_error (about 0.03) are both low, so this is not a jitter or gain-oscillation failure. The robot is being thrown off the line early in each run, and the single line-loss event that begins before the failure points to a detection problem.\n\nDiagnosis: the sensor span is too narrow. With 12 mm sensors at x = +/-0.02, a sensor only sees tape when the line is within about 0.0185 m of its x-position, so once the robot drifts about 2 cm off centre every sensor reads near zero. The sum drops below 0.05, the line is lost, and last_direction then turns the robot at full authority, which pushes it past the 6 cm limit. The outer sensors at x = +/-0.02 also barely overlap the 25 mm tape even when the robot is centred.\n\nChanges (one group of geometry, plus a conservative speed/gain adjustment):\n1. Geometry: sensor_size 0.016 and five sensors at x = 0.03, 0.015, 0, -0.015, -0.03, all at y = 0.03. The line is then detectable out to about +/-0.045 m, which is inside the 0.06 m limit, and adjacent sensors overlap so the signal stays continuous. Weights stay [1.0, 0.5, 0, -0.5, -1.0], matching the new x-order.\n2. Controller: base_speed 1.6 to 1.3. The race died in the first fraction of the course, which suggests the robot is too fast to recover from an early error; a slower pace should reduce slip and overshoot. The completion-time penalty is small compared with off-track termination.\n3. kp 3.0 to 2.5 and kd 0.05 to 0.1. With wider sensors the signal is less saturated, so a slightly lower kp avoids overcorrection, and a little more derivative damps the response to the larger signal change.\n\nExpected result: line_loss_events falls toward 0, runs progress further (the goal is progress above 0.5, then completion), and max_error stays below 0.06. Completion within 3 s may still be out of reach at 1.3 m/s, and I expect the next limit to be speed or completion time, not off-track.\n\nHypotheses to test next: (H1) if line_loss_events is still above 0, the detection span is still short and I should add a sixth sensor or switch line_loss to search_left or search_right, depending on the side the line is lost. (H2) If progress is high but completion is 0 with completion_time near 3 s, raise base_speed to 1.6 to 1.8. (H3) If steering_oscillation rises, lower kp further and keep kd near 0.1 to 0.15.",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": 0.03, "y": 0.03},
      {"x": 0.015, "y": 0.03},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.03},
      {"x": -0.015, "y": 0.03},
      {"x": -0.03, "y": 0.03}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.016,
    "wheel_track": 0.12,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "body_length": 0.18,
    "mass": 0.8
  },
  "controller": {
    "base_speed": 1.3,
    "kp": 2.5,
    "ki": 0.0,
    "kd": 0.1,
    "sensor_weights": [1.0, 0.5, 0.0, -0.5, -1.0],
    "line_loss": "last_direction"
  },
  "observation_request": {
    "measurements": [
      "completion",
      "progress",
      "completion_time",
      "rms_error",
      "max_error",
      "line_loss_events",
      "steering_oscillation",
      "control_effort",
      "controller_errors",
      "noise_robustness"
    ]
  },
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Wider sensors, slower base",
      "notes": "Widens the sensor span to x = ±0.03 with 16 mm sensors so the line stays detectable out to about ±0.045 m, and lowers base_speed to 1.3 with kp 2.5 and kd 0.1. Iteration 0 went off track in every trial with one line-loss event each, which points to the narrow sensor array losing the line; the change is aimed at that."
    }
  }
}
